00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:02.370
This is an exciting day

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.223
to have the Ghana panel publicly released.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.740
So, there was a time that
high quality data from low

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.503
and middle income countries
was really scarce.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.730
W. Arthur Lewis and Ester
Boserup could change the way

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.930
we looked at the world by
having a few tables of data

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.133
from customs records and a
few anthropology case studies.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.630
This weekend makes it abundantly clear

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.760
that that's just not true anymore.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.593
The rich data

00:05:34.895 --> 00:05:37.181
(microphone back feed high pitched noise)

00:05:37.181 --> 00:05:39.740
the rich data that's available from RCTs,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.890
expenditure surveys,
from administrative data,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.333
from remote sensing data.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.190
Their availability is
facilitating really rapid progress

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.740
in how we understand the world.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:54.700
In this

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:56.723
setting

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:58.713
of rich data,

00:05:59.822 --> 00:06:02.700
as Rohini actually started
to mention, what's the use

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:03.533
of these

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.730
big

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.190
household panel survey datas?

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.690
Data sets.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.473
When Mark and I started
thinking about this,

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.710
our goal was to

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.730
set up a lab

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.730
in which people could think
about development in new ways.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.510
We thought that by committing

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.240
to a very long term comprehensive

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.530
and coordinated set of studies
that obtain individual's,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.270
that obtain data on individual's life

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.090
course as they move through

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.460
the process of development and the built,

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.550
and natural environment in
which they're operating,

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.140
we could provide tools that would lead

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.200
to unexpected insights,
that we'd find things

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.143
that we didn't know were happening.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.583
And so, that's what I'm hoping
these data can be used for.

00:06:54.474 --> 00:06:55.307
And we have

00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:57.990
I actually have two slides I wanna show.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:58.823
Yeah.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.480
Blank one.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.480
(audience laughing)

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.711
- [Rohini] No, you got to go back.

00:07:08.711 --> 00:07:09.880
- No, that's what I want.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:10.713
- Okay, great.

00:07:11.887 --> 00:07:12.720
- Okay.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.620
So, this is what panel data
gets you fundamentally.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.620
It gets you what's happening
to an individual over time.

00:07:22.847 --> 00:07:24.390
And so that's just a scatter plot

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:25.223
of

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.660
food expenditure

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.150
per adult equivalent in households,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.540
in wave one and wave three
of the Ghana panel data.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.770
So, those are separated
by, for a 12 years.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.410
And so there's guys in the upper left

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:44.280
who started off quite poor
and are now doing very well.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.960
There's guys in the lower right
who were doing pretty well

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.328
in wave one and are doing badly now.

00:07:51.328 --> 00:07:52.867
What distinguishes them?

00:07:52.867 --> 00:07:54.813
What's driving this?

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.320
And so, that's the sort
of fundamental thing

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.880
that we know about from panel data.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.200
We can track people over time
and see what's happening.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.760
What

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.560
is unique about

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:08.393
the data sets

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:10.830
that we'll be talking about today

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:15.000
is the breadth of information that we have

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.923
about these same individuals.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:18.773
So,

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.020
Sam and Andre are gonna talk to you

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:23.670
about some of the specifics.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.590
Let me just give you an example.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.247
We asked people,

00:08:27.247 --> 00:08:30.000
"what do you think about when
you fall asleep at night?"

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.222
And they told us, and so,

00:08:33.222 --> 00:08:36.030
here's

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:37.620
the words

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.300
that come up most

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:44.300
for people in households that
have done better over time,

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.460
that have gone from that lower right,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.753
that are in the upper left of
that scatter plot they had.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:56.850
And here's the words that were

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:57.683
mentioned more

00:08:57.683 --> 00:08:59.823
by the ones who were falling.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.090
And I don't know if this means anything,

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.280
this is not actually something
that I'm gonna study.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.880
But the point is that by
drawing together information

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.870
from

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.460
various

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:14.430
widely dispersed parts of people's lives,

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.530
we might make unexpected connections.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.350
This is gonna come up later, I think,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:20.730
when we have a discussion.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:24.030
But let me now turn it over to

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:24.863
Sam.

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:26.230
- All right, thank you Chris.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:33.060
So, I have a text to introduce
briefly the "Ghana Panel"

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.260
and I'll do that with Andre.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.847
We have five minutes each so I would skip

00:09:36.847 --> 00:09:38.640
or not go into so much details.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.140
We can talk about that later.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.000
So, this is the outline we are using.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.968
So, we would look at the
sampling design and attrition,

00:09:44.968 --> 00:09:48.090
the instruments and then topics covered

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:50.880
under the "Ghana Panel", and
then the field procedures.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:52.080
And then Andre would come in

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.940
with some descriptive statistics

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.600
and some exploratory transition analysis.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:58.433
Okay,

00:09:58.433 --> 00:09:59.340
so

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:00.480
so the "Ghana Panel" follows

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.480
a two-stage sampling procedure.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.750
The first stage was the

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.130
selection of clusters,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.170
334 clusters.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:11.880
And in each of these clusters,

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.920
we had 15 households selected.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.090
So, that gave a total of 5,010

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.140
households.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:21.250
Okay, so there's a unique feature

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:23.610
I would want to highlight here,

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.550
which is we track moved households

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:28.530
and split-off household members.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.483
That is breakaway household members.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.480
I'm sure you might be,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.000
there's something puzzling going on there.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.293
Why would we have 5,009

00:10:38.293 --> 00:10:40.890
4,774

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:41.723
and then

00:10:41.723 --> 00:10:43.920
5,669

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.150
you might be guessing.

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:48.120
So the next slide would
show us what is happening.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.103
So you find that indeed in
the wave one we had 5,009,

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.420
surveyed households. But
moving to the wave two,

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.020
we find that of that sample

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:00.853
643

00:11:02.228 --> 00:11:04.020
attrited for various reasons it

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:07.830
could be the death of all the
household members refusal,

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:11.520
it could be migration outside
Ghana or we were just not able

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.590
to reach these households.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:14.423
Okay, so

00:11:14.423 --> 00:11:15.256
that was it.

00:11:15.256 --> 00:11:19.680
And so you also find that for
the split of household members

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.260
we had 965

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.180
but we don't trace all of them.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.380
We don't follow all of them.

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.080
So we randomly select some to follow.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:29.580
Okay, so that is how come

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.150
we still ended up with the 4,774 but we

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:34.350
were, the same thing happened.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:39.030
But we're fortunate for the
third way to have located 396

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:43.230
households who were not
located in the second wave.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:47.280
So that is how come we have
a greater than number for the

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.470
third wave. So I'll move on.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.150
Yeah, so the instrument,

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:55.440
we started using computer
assisted personal interviewing

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.550
since the wave two.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.860
The first wave was a paper based

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.720
and for the wave two we used Blaze.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.294
And beyond that we use service
CTO for both the wave three

00:12:05.294 --> 00:12:06.576
and then four.

00:12:06.576 --> 00:12:07.409
Okay,

00:12:07.409 --> 00:12:11.377
so here are some topics
you can find in the

00:12:11.377 --> 00:12:13.110
"Ghana Panel", and

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:14.880
we for the fourth wave,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.530
we have introduced some new
questions which I mean we find

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:20.910
relevant in recent times.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:25.380
So Ghana recently introduced
some policies and we think it

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:29.310
best to include such questions.
So an example is the,

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:31.890
is this an electronic transaction levy?

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.640
So we wanted to know what people
thought of it and then some

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.890
feedback cause this
was very controversial.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.020
And then there's also the
planting for food and jobs.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.685
So we ask questions also on
that and we also include social

00:12:42.685 --> 00:12:44.760
media questions.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:46.470
Okay, so if anyone is also interested

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:47.970
in our internet speed data,

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.700
we have it in there. So um,

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.200
quickly the field procedure,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.990
I'll just run through it cause
I have just two minutes left.

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:58.380
So like I said, we have the
household tracking, we did,

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.570
this is mainly for the
wave four, we did this.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:05.070
First we used phones to track
the households to know if they

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:08.520
are still where they are before
we went on to look for them.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:09.900
So that is what proceed,

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.230
I mean start we started with
and then we had the teams and

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:16.350
then we put them together and
then we do these before these

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:17.880
protocols. We do a district entry,

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.730
community entry and then
an in person tracking

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:23.760
once the

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.900
teams are in the communities.
So I would just skip to also,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.760
once they submit the data,

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.010
we go through some quality checks.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.833
We can think of high
frequency checks. We do that,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.460
it seems like a real
time data quality check.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:40.620
So once they submit that data to us,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:44.850
we go through and then check
for potential outliers.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.490
For instance, there are
times for several reasons,

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:50.700
tiredness you can, you
could find age being 3000,

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:53.580
that doesn't really sound

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:54.413
right.

00:13:54.413 --> 00:13:56.790
So we prompt them and
then we go through this.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.777
And then we also have the back
checks where we call back the

00:13:59.777 --> 00:14:04.440
survey households to ask same
some questions. For instance,

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.420
we could ask the household,

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:10.170
we could ask for the number
of plots and just to see if it

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:11.580
corresponds with what the,

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.070
we have in the data
submitted by the field team.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:17.160
And also we have the
high frequent, I'm sorry,

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:21.093
we had a fields monitoring
here. So this study is,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:23.078
we started integrating our CTs into the

00:14:23.078 --> 00:14:25.860
"Ghana Panel"

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:27.810
starting with a third wave.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.780
And this is one study which
I think creates and then some

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:33.180
other cohorts did.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.730
So I am particularly
interested in this or I find it

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.850
exciting because what it
does is that it shows how

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:41.970
livelihood support joint
pandemics could lead people to

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:44.730
adhere to public health directives.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.250
And so in this case it use
the COVID 19 and then social

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.680
distancing. Okay, so the question is,

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:51.300
if we

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.442
give these monies to people,

00:14:53.442 --> 00:14:55.800
are they likely to social distance?

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:57.042
And it finds positive

00:14:57.042 --> 00:14:59.220
effects. So I'll turn it over to Andre now

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:00.810
for him to go through.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:02.219
Thank you.

00:15:02.219 --> 00:15:05.219
