00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:12.450
So thanks very much.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.480
It's been a great conference
so far and I'm just gonna use

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:18.450
the next few minutes to talk
about a few of the types of

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:22.140
exploratory data analysis that
can be very quickly, readily,

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:25.200
easily done with "Ghana Panel" data.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.250
So we decided to take three
example variables that represent

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:30.923
different types of, you know, different.

00:15:30.923 --> 00:15:34.020
(high pitched feedback)

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.090
First we have the food
consumption per capital,

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.340
which is the sum across all food types.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.260
Of purchased,

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.080
received as gift and produced food.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:47.040
Then we have the probability
of poverty index as a proxy for

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.240
poverty,

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.210
which gives the percentage
probability that a household

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.380
falls below a particular
say, prosperity threshold.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.840
In this case we're using
the national poverty line.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.750
And then third, the "Kessler
Distress Scale", sorry,

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:04.260
the probability of poverty
index comes from 10 questions of

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.650
assets, household size,
that kind of thing.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:10.230
The Kessler has 10 questions
about how often in the past

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:15.210
month people have felt, you
know, depressed, anxious,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:16.043
et cetera.

00:16:16.043 --> 00:16:18.780
And the idea is that it's a
non-clinical construct that's

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:22.500
associated with clinical
constructs like anxiety and

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:23.790
depression.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:24.623
So is,

00:16:24.623 --> 00:16:27.017
you can see for food
consumption went way up between,

00:16:27.017 --> 00:16:29.953
oh and food consumptions
also adjusted for CPI,

00:16:29.953 --> 00:16:33.330
I should mention, went way
up between waves one and two,

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.660
but then back down waves two and three.

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:41.010
Similarly the Kessler went
down between waves one and two,

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:43.260
which is good, that means less distress.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.680
Then back up just a little
wave three PPI went down

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:47.820
consistently,

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.970
but only a little bit
between waves two and three.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.040
So that we could have
seen from across sections.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:56.520
But to take up some of the
themes that Chris was showing in

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:58.200
his graph,

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.870
we can also look at poverty
transitions or just more

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:04.440
generally development trajectories
and stories of particular

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.620
households and understanding
what's associated with upward

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:09.960
mobility and downward mobility.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.450
So here for each of the three variables,

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.870
we reduce the sample to only
households that we can link

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.703
between waves one and three.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:22.350
And then we take above and
below median on the outcome in

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:25.590
question for waves one,
same for wave three.

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.660
And because it's gonna
be symmetrical here,

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:32.010
we're only showing the
below median in wave one.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:35.190
So this also gives a chance
to compare mobility of these

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:38.040
three outcomes as food consumption.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.650
You can see there's a
good amount of mobility.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:41.940
PPI not so much,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:46.770
79% of the households who
were below median in wave one

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.680
stayed below median in wave three.

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.350
And then Kessler also more mobility.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.110
So of course we can't say for
sure what's measurement error

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.330
and what's actually capturing things.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:01.230
PPI should be more precise,
but also because of the,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.120
the types of questions asked.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.070
but also since it involves durable assets,

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.020
there's likely something real going on.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.520
So now to break this
out, just one more step.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:14.670
One of the biggest social
distinctions we could say in Ghana

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:17.220
is North versus South
social and geographical and

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:18.475
agroecological.

00:18:18.475 --> 00:18:20.847
So there's a lot going on here.

00:18:20.847 --> 00:18:25.110
And so we want to see is mobility
higher for Northern or for

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:26.610
Southern, et cetera.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:27.443
So.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.410
So now starting with food consumption,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:34.620
we can see that in the South
below median there's a good

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:35.880
amount of mobility.

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:38.790
A lot of people in, who are
below median in South in wave

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:41.670
one were above median by wave three.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:44.460
But the situation was not
so great for below median

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.140
households in the North.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:50.340
They were overwhelmingly still
below median by wave three,

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:51.840
12 years later.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:55.050
And then a similar story
for above and below.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:58.230
Now we're not controlling for
regional price differences,

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.120
so that could be part of this.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.910
But as we'll see the pattern's
the same for PPI for below

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:06.030
median, not so much for above median.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.460
So this is a much starker
table as you can see,

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:13.020
especially point you towards
the second row where 96% of the

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:16.920
households who are below
median in the PPI index in wave

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.330
one, stay below median wave

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:21.390
three.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:24.480
And so then psychological
distress, on the other hand,

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:25.560
there's a lot more movement.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.200
And so of course we're thinking
not only about the actual

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:29.033
trends,

00:19:29.033 --> 00:19:30.810
but about how these different
variables are capturing

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.900
different constructs that
might vary more quickly in real

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:36.500
life. And we're thinking
about potential threats to,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:41.550
you know, measurement error
and other validity concerns.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.550
So these are some density
plots like Chris showed.

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:47.100
And you can see on left we have the South,

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:48.720
most of the households are above the line,

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:51.960
meaning that they had experienced
upward mobility and it's

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:55.650
the opposite for the South. So
I'll actually wrap up there.

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:58.530
There are some other interesting
findings with farms and

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:00.053
plots, but yeah,

00:20:00.053 --> 00:20:02.760
so we very much hope that you'll
use the data and share the

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:06.840
results with us and maybe
present them next year at

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.132
any UDC. So thank you very much.

00:20:09.132 --> 00:20:12.132
(audience applause)

