- [Rohini Pande] Graciela
we'll now turn to you

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:17.970
to talk about their experiences in

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:19.470
Mexico.
- Sure.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:20.303
- Can you put the,

00:20:20.303 --> 00:20:22.070
- Yeah, the slide is actually coming up.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:24.780
- Well, thank you very much

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:26.790
for the invitation to be here.

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:30.720
I'm gonna be talking about
poverty measurement in Mexico and

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:34.680
specifically using the
"Mexican Family Live Survey".

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.780
So um,

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:37.613
one second.

00:20:38.817 --> 00:20:40.196
How do I do it?

00:20:40.196 --> 00:20:42.100
- That that was the
- The green one.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:43.833
- Oh, there, its okay.

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:46.763
All right.

00:20:46.763 --> 00:20:50.790
So poverty measurement in
Mexico official is done by

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:51.623
CONEVAL,

00:20:51.623 --> 00:20:54.360
the Mexican council for the
evaluation of social policy.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:55.350
And they have a mandate,

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:59.580
it has to be done every two years at the,

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:02.400
at the national level
and at the state level.

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:05.400
And they have to use official
data coming from the National

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:07.110
Bureau of Statistics.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:12.110
And the indicators have to be
decomposable and it has the,

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.530
the National Bureau of
Statistics produces that income

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:19.770
expenditure survey, which is
a series of cross sections.

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:23.850
So what we use is actually
that information to produce the

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:25.170
official measurements.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:28.440
So this is basically the
methodology that we use.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:32.250
So we have on the X
axis social deprivation,

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:34.440
which goes from the right
to the left if you have

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:37.290
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 social deprivation.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:41.670
And on the y axis you have
basically income economic

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:42.503
wellbeing.

00:21:42.503 --> 00:21:45.180
So the interaction between
these two dimensions is what

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.130
gives you poverty.

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:51.230
So extreme poverty is
the red box and it's,

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:54.690
it basically is three
deprivations or more and below a

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:56.820
minimum income level.

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:01.820
And it's basically 10% of the
population across all years in

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.710
the last 10 years is basically the same.

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:07.350
And then we have moderate poverty,

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:10.020
which is if you have at least
one deprivation and you're

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.140
below this upper income level,

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:16.950
so you're moderately poor
and it's basically 36% of the

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:18.930
population in that quadrant.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:23.280
And then the rest of the country
is divided into non-poor,

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:28.080
which is the green quadrant,
which is basically 19%.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.630
And then you have the
vulnerable by income,

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:32.940
which is like 5% of the population,

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:36.510
which is those below
the income poverty line,

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:40.440
but no social deprivation
or the vulnerable by social

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:43.380
deprivations, which are
those that have high,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:47.250
high income but have
some sort of deprivation.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:50.082
So basically if you look at
poverty measurements in Mexico,

00:22:50.082 --> 00:22:51.720
the, the indicators,

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:55.530
they don't typically change across time.

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:58.533
And so the question is,

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:02.850
we don't know anything
about poverty transition.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:07.850
So we see 10%, 10%, 10% of
people being in extreme poverty.

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:12.270
And the question is whether
there's actually no mobility out

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:13.103
of poverty,

00:23:13.103 --> 00:23:16.110
are people living in extreme
poverty able to ever get out of

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.880
poverty or not?

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.060
So we're gonna use the "MXFLS",

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:24.030
which is this longitudinal study,

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:27.180
to try to answer and look
at some of this questions.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.820
So if a person is born poor,

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:31.067
will she

00:23:31.067 --> 00:23:31.900
or he

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:32.733
die

00:23:32.733 --> 00:23:33.660
being poor?

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.960
Is there social mobility as
measured by these changes in

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:38.640
poverty status?

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.860
And is there a way out of poverty?

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:43.170
So I'm going to present some of the,

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:46.680
exercise that we did to try
to answer this question.

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:49.650
So we're gonna use the
Mexican Family Life Survey,

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.040
which is the only

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:54.180
nationally representative

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:55.013
panel

00:23:55.013 --> 00:23:58.890
multi topic purpose survey in Mexico.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:02.550
And it allows to study
associations between different

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:05.400
indicators of welfare because
it has everything you can

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:06.870
think of, I'm sure it has.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:08.653
So you can go into the web, I'm not,

00:24:08.653 --> 00:24:10.400
I I don't have a lot of time to,

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.700
to go into a lot of details,
but it collects biomarkers,

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.943
objective and subjective
indicators of health,

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:20.039
interviews, everyone

00:24:20.039 --> 00:24:23.850
in the household as opposed
to the representative

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:24.960
respondent.

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:28.080
And it also collects information
about the community prices

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.523
and infrastructure at
the local level. It's a,

00:24:30.523 --> 00:24:32.250
a longitudinal survey, as I said,

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:35.280
it relocates and re-interviews baseline,

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:38.460
the baseline sample collected in 2002

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:39.810
and it

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:40.643
tracks

00:24:40.643 --> 00:24:44.580
individuals both within Mexico
and into the United States.

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:45.990
So there's three waves.

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.713
We actually field it started
fielding a fourth wave in,

00:24:53.642 --> 00:24:55.170
2018,

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:56.883
but we,

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.660
it's really painful to say this but,

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:03.480
the government actually cut
funding and we couldn't even

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:05.070
finish the, the fourth wave.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:08.190
So it's basically like half done.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:09.600
Approximately,

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:13.500
we started collecting 35,000
individuals in the baseline and

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:16.630
we're about 40,000 respondents

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:18.870
by the last wave.

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:22.770
So I'm not gonna talk about
this because I don't have a lot

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.330
of

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:25.163
time.

00:25:25.163 --> 00:25:27.240
The only thing that I'm
gonna say is that, why are

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:31.020
longitudinal data useful for
the study of measurement?

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:32.250
So as Chris was saying,

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:35.070
is obviously to track individuals
across time and to see,

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:37.470
look at transitions in and out of poverty,

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.630
that's one advantage.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:43.110
And another one is that since
it's the same households

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:44.640
across time,

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:48.570
you can use information from
different waves to complement

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:52.906
and to, to, to complement the study

00:25:52.906 --> 00:25:55.530
of property measurement. So for example,

00:25:55.530 --> 00:26:00.000
we use expenditure as opposed to income

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.770
because we followed

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:05.610
these people along the
years that we, we thought,

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.000
and we proved that it was
better to use expenditure when

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.790
using poverty measurements.
But also, for example,

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:14.790
we didn't have information about
food deprivation in all the

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:16.560
waves, only in the third wave.

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:19.800
So we used that information to
model food deprivation in the

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:23.070
first two waves. So that's
one of the advantages.

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:24.630
So I'm not gonna stop on this.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:27.720
So what are the social
deprivations? Access to education,

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:29.972
health, social security, housing, basic,

00:26:29.972 --> 00:26:32.403
basic services and food.

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:38.690
So we're gonna use this
definition of poverty

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:40.290
in Mexico

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:42.360
and well, I'm just gonna show you, ,

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:44.280
I cannot really stop here.

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:48.690
But if you use MXFLS as a
cross section and compare to

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.290
what indicators are obtained
using the CONEVAL efficient

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:57.090
measures, the numbers are
basically very similar.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:01.290
So we're going to use for this
exercise that I'm gonna show

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:04.740
you, we're gonna classify
poverty into four groups.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:07.230
The first is chronic poverty.

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:11.940
So those are that in the three
waves we're always classified

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.200
as poor.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:15.210
Then we have the persistent poverty.

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:19.680
So comprised of people who
experience poverty in two of the

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:20.970
three

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:22.140
waves

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:23.820
and transitory poverty,

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:26.727
those who were

00:27:26.727 --> 00:27:27.600
classified

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:28.950
as four in one

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:32.070
and then none poor,
never classified as poor.

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:32.910
So,

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.330
so we basically have all these
kinds of combinations across

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:38.130
the three waves.

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:42.670
And so what are the results
that we get? So this is

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:47.910
how is, how is it distributed
among these four groups?

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:52.830
So it's basically 25% almost
equally distributed between the

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:54.510
four groups.

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:57.150
And another way of reading it

00:27:57.150 --> 00:27:58.170
is that

00:27:58.170 --> 00:27:59.760
75%

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:00.630
of the population

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:04.290
in Mexico, in this 10
years of the study period,

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:06.630
have been poor at least once.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:07.800
So this is a,

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:09.750
different way of
interpreting these results.

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:13.590
And if you look at, for
example, the extreme poverty,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.720
which is the, the lowest one,

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:19.500
extreme chronic poverty
is less than 2% in Mexico,

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:24.090
extreme persistent poverty,
5% transitory, 16%.

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:28.350
So another way of putting it
is that one out of every five

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:31.219
people in Mexico have been in
extreme poverty in this last,

00:28:31.219 --> 00:28:35.359
in this study period of 10 years.

00:28:35.359 --> 00:28:39.150
So,

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.520
people, if you look at, for example,

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:46.230
at the official poverty
measurements in, in Mexico,

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:47.130
those are,

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:51.960
those official indicators are
used to delineate or design

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:53.430
public policies.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:57.540
So public policies based on
those first scraps that I show

00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:00.780
you, or those first statistics
guide you to very different

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:05.730
policies as if you used
these longitudinal or

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:06.660
panel

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:07.493
results.

00:29:07.493 --> 00:29:08.890
So the

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:12.180
conclusions would be different.

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:16.590
So public policy is currently
designed by using the static

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:17.423
groups.

00:29:17.423 --> 00:29:20.580
So policies aimed at helping
these groups may be focused

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:25.080
only on short term poverty alleviation,

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:28.350
and not looking at the
structural causes of poverty.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:30.810
So that's something that can be done.

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:32.880
I'm not going to stop at this.

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:36.910
I will share my slides later
with you guys so you can see

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:42.150
what the data looks like in
term of the chronicity of

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:43.320
poverty.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:46.260
But determinants of current
poverty differ significantly

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:49.233
from determinants of transitory poverty.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:52.260
Sorry.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:53.509
And they're very,

00:29:53.509 --> 00:29:56.370
they're very different depending
obviously on the income

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:58.890
poverty line that you use.

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:01.830
Poverty in general is associated
with a number of adults in

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:04.320
the households because those
are the ones working and those

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:07.410
are the ones that are generating income.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:11.400
But specifically chronicity
of poverty is very much

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:14.250
associated with schooling
and health conditions.

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:17.580
So more schooling and
better health leads to lower

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:21.570
chronicity of poverty as one would expect.

00:30:21.570 --> 00:30:25.560
So employment and particularly
former employment are the

00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:29.010
cornerstone for permanent
poverty reduction.

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:31.470
Let me just go to the last slide,

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.078
which talks about the lessons learned.

00:30:34.078 --> 00:30:37.380
And I would say that
longitudinal approaches

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:39.600
give more information

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:44.340
and information that is more
suited in order to delineate or

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:47.910
design protected public policies.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:52.830
This multipurpose component
also adds a lot because you can

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:55.580
use a lot of, of breadth of,

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:59.180
of social demographic indicators to,

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:03.300
to design these antipoverty projects.

00:31:03.300 --> 00:31:04.740
And

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:05.573
typically,

00:31:05.573 --> 00:31:08.610
and this is something that I
will just be my my last comment

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:11.760
that I think is something very
nice for you guys working on

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:15.900
these topic is that a
lot of our countries,

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:19.623
especially in Latin
America, are suffering,

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:22.890
are suffering from our
democracies, are suffering,

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:25.140
and our we're shaking.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:30.140
And also the data collection
is shaking in our countries.

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:34.020
And there's some countries
in Latin America that are not

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:35.280
being able to collect data,

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:38.520
to study poverty or to
study anything else.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:39.353
So it's,

00:31:39.353 --> 00:31:42.453
it's a very good idea for efforts,

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.720
that efforts can be done

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:48.780
from the academia, academic
point of view to collect this

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:53.190
kind of data so that we can
produce poverty estimates and we

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:58.190
can compare poverty estimates
over time. So I'll stop here.

