00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:21.270
Okay, so this the "Cox Bazaar
Panel Survey", "CBPS" it's,

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:24.330
focused on a very unusual
and very specific population.

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:25.380
The Rohingya refugees,

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:28.140
we had a large influx of Rohingya
refugees from Myanmar into

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:29.730
Bangladesh in 2017.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:31.650
There were lots of Rohingyas before that.

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:33.420
And actually we started a,

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:36.180
a data collection process
with them even prior to 2017.

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:39.090
And then suddenly in the
middle of preparing, you know,

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:42.840
we had close to a million people
who showed up in the matter

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.930
of a couple of months in a
country that wasn't very,

00:33:45.930 --> 00:33:48.363
well prepared to handle
such a large influx.

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:53.910
And, and so, and at that point
to Yale leadership's credit,

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.400
everybody was thinking about,
okay, how do we contribute?

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:57.233
And that's, we,

00:33:57.233 --> 00:34:00.390
we decided that maybe we should
start asking some questions

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:02.970
and collecting some information
that might be useful for

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:03.930
policy, right?

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:06.240
And so that led to identifying, you know,

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:09.990
we need to understand what's
happening with this particular

00:34:09.990 --> 00:34:11.370
population, right?

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.443
That's important because I
mean, there was gonna be a,

00:34:14.443 --> 00:34:16.830
a case in the International
Court of Justice, right?

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.440
And it was important that
their voices be heard because

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:24.210
Aung San Suu Kyi was presenting
the Myanmar voice, right?

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:27.720
And so we needed systematic
data and then the Myanmar's

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:30.660
commentary always was, Oh, these
like journalistic accounts,

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:31.920
they're not representative, et cetera.

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:34.257
So we wanted to get some
representative information about

00:34:34.257 --> 00:34:38.550
the trauma that the Rohingya
had had, had experienced.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:40.710
But it was also clear to me
as soon as I started visiting

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:44.400
the area that what happened
in five years or 10 years to

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.110
this population, initially in 2017,

00:34:46.110 --> 00:34:49.290
everybody was just focused
on how do we provide the most

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:52.830
basic services, just food and
shelter, sanitation, water,

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:53.852
et cetera, right?

00:34:53.852 --> 00:34:56.670
Everybody was just focused on
that as they should have been.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.100
That's, that's what
was needed immediately.

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:01.819
But, but it was also clear
to me that five or 10 years,

00:35:01.819 --> 00:35:06.300
what happens to this population
over time will depend on how

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.370
local politics changes, right?

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:10.440
So the Bangladesh government,
made our, prime minister

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:11.273
made a very

00:35:11.273 --> 00:35:13.590
brave decision at that point that yeah,

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:14.880
of course we are going to welcome them.

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:17.973
We were refugees once, which
is true only 50 years ago.

00:35:17.973 --> 00:35:21.360
And, and so it's our
responsibility to make room,

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:25.890
but as soon as people start
getting impatient with their

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.080
presence, because the
people who live in the area,

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:30.810
they themselves are
deprived and poor, right?

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:33.720
So if they start getting impatient
and the politics changes,

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:36.180
that creates a much more
dangerous situation, right?

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:39.060
So therefore we decided to
do the survey not only of the

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:42.390
Rohingya, but what we
call the host community,

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:44.940
so people who were already living there,

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:48.480
and then there's a third
population of pre 2017 Rohingya

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:49.950
arrivals who were trying to, they were,

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:51.420
it was illegal for them to be there,

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.210
but they were trying to then
blend into the population,

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:55.740
but also to have a contrast,

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:58.230
not only the host community
that was immediately surrounding

00:35:58.230 --> 00:35:59.063
the camps,

00:35:59.063 --> 00:36:02.130
but host communities that were
farther away from the camps

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:04.013
just to have that contrast as well. Yeah.

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.250
And um,

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:09.450
so now let me,

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:12.750
now get into the details
of the data itself. So,

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:13.800
we decided to,

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.100
in order to create a sampling
frame and then have a sample

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:20.400
of 5,000 people that we would
follow around in a panel,

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:21.233
right?

00:36:21.233 --> 00:36:25.190
We first had to have touchpoints
like kind of a sampling or,

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:27.240
or like a census type exercise.

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:30.870
So we had to have touchpoints
with about 55,000 people or

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:33.330
households. So what we
ended up doing was, okay,

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:36.603
so from those 55,000, we'll
choose these 5,000 to,

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:40.470
to survey intensively over time.

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:43.200
And the other 50,000 was we,

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:46.050
we just made it available
to researchers saying, look,

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:47.910
if you, here's a sampling frame,

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:49.636
we've already done that exercise,

00:36:49.636 --> 00:36:52.710
that's something that
might be costly, right?

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:55.380
And we would welcome
anybody who has ideas on,

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:57.630
let's say they want to run
interventions that might be

00:36:57.630 --> 00:36:59.250
beneficial to this population,

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:03.090
whether it's cash transfers or
psychosocial work, et cetera.

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:07.350
So researchers can come in
and use that sampling frame.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:09.510
And if you want to do
intervention based experimental

00:37:09.510 --> 00:37:11.940
research, you're allowed
to do that, right?

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:12.773
And,

00:37:12.773 --> 00:37:16.020
and it's just the 5,000 households
that we wouldn't want to

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:19.380
start running interventions in
that sample because then they

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:21.870
would cease to be representative, right?

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:23.490
And that would undermine. So, so we,

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:27.330
we created these two subpopulations
50,000 of people who we

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:29.037
could run interventions
on versus not, right?

00:37:29.037 --> 00:37:30.690
And the data are all publicly available,

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:33.570
I hope people choose
to use it. We are now,

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.810
we did one round of intensive
sort of in-person surveying,

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:38.490
and then we did multiple during Covid,

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:41.384
we did multiple rounds of
phone surveys to continue also

00:37:41.384 --> 00:37:44.130
maintaining touchpoint so that
we could more easily track

00:37:44.130 --> 00:37:45.270
them. As you can imagine,

00:37:45.270 --> 00:37:47.280
this is a population that's
gonna be very difficult for us

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:48.780
to track over time,

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:50.670
precisely because they're
mobile and they don't want to be

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.370
there. They, they'd like to
get out as soon as they can.

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:54.203
And in fact,

00:37:54.203 --> 00:37:56.430
the government relocated a
lot of them in a politically

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:00.303
contentious decision to shore
area, like a island type area.

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.860
And um,

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:05.693
Okay.

00:38:05.693 --> 00:38:08.861
And then the other benefit of, you know,

00:38:08.861 --> 00:38:11.490
having these two sub-populations
is that if you end up

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:14.460
running interventions in
like the, some, you know,

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:17.010
in some sub sample of the 50,000 people,

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:21.780
then with nice thing is that
you have 5,000, our sample

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:24.130
households, the panel
households who you could,

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:27.720
who you could use as a very,
very large control group,

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:30.810
right? So then if you run
experiments on, you know,

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:33.750
you choose 200 people or 500
people to run an experiment on

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:35.640
somewhere else, you have,
have you automatically,

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:39.510
as long as we maintain
measurement consistently in

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.730
measurement, then you could
use, you could use that.

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:46.350
So again, everyone's welcome to come and,

00:38:46.350 --> 00:38:47.403
and do research here.

00:38:49.590 --> 00:38:51.570
So yeah. One other thing,

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:54.660
one other point I'll mention
about the importance of the,

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:55.680
of a panel.

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:59.100
So there was an unintended
benefit during the Covid period,

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:02.580
which is that I learned that
like during Covid suddenly,

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:03.600
of course we had to all,

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:06.030
everybody had to stop our
in-person data collection

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:07.641
activities. But thankfully we had,

00:39:07.641 --> 00:39:11.183
along with this sample and
other areas in Bangladesh,

00:39:11.183 --> 00:39:15.150
we had a sample of people
whose statistical properties we

00:39:15.150 --> 00:39:16.380
had some understanding of.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.780
So we knew what adjustments
we'd have to make,

00:39:18.780 --> 00:39:22.440
plus we had their phone numbers
and we also knew that the

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:25.920
phones were picked up only
three, four months before, right?

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:29.100
And so that allowed us to
quickly in March, 2020 itself

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:34.100
start doing high frequent high
frequency data collection for

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:38.911
these populations. And, and
so that, so for example,

00:39:38.911 --> 00:39:42.630
actually across the Ghana survey
and other countries and the

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:45.000
Bangladesh surveys, we
ended up writing a paper.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:47.070
So even though Chris and I
were colleagues for a decade,

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:48.810
we never managed to
write a paper together.

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:52.260
He left and during the
Covid period we did, right?

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:57.260
Thanks, thanks to all this data
and okay, so one, you know,

00:39:57.278 --> 00:40:02.278
so one like sort of tangible
example of how the panel

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:03.480
dimension helps.

00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:05.910
Now it turns out there's a
lot of seasonality, right?

00:40:05.910 --> 00:40:09.030
Which is not a surprise to
any, any development economist,

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:09.863
like within,

00:40:09.863 --> 00:40:12.960
within year there's a lot of
variation in people's outcomes.

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:16.410
And so Covid just happened
to hit in some countries

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:18.180
it happened to hit during a good period,

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:20.880
like a harvest period where
people had grain stocks in other

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:22.980
countries there were the
pre-harvest period, right?

00:40:22.980 --> 00:40:25.590
And so we were learning,
thankfully, once you have the,

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:28.740
a lot of the pre Covid data, right?

00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:30.330
You could track, you know,

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:31.710
as long as it was high frequency enough,

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:34.890
you could track what kind of
seasonal variation there was.

00:40:34.890 --> 00:40:37.200
And that allowed us to say
that, look in this country,

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:40.320
maybe people aren't suffering
so much right now. Right?

00:40:40.320 --> 00:40:43.410
But that's not good news because
relative to the same season

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:46.890
in a previous year, it was
actually really bad news, right?

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:49.173
This is why also I think when
we all contribute to these

00:40:49.173 --> 00:40:50.490
kind of data, public goods,

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:53.520
they sometimes pay off dividends for,

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:55.923
for us in unanticipated ways.

00:40:58.080 --> 00:41:01.320
And the final thing I'll
mention is about partnerships.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:04.710
So even though Yale was willing
to, for the first round,

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:07.680
was willing to pay for
the survey themselves,

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.910
we ended up partnering with
the World Bank and UNHCR.

00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:15.420
And that's been very useful
because those two organizations,

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:17.070
and other than funds and money,

00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:19.800
they also have really good
convening power, right?

00:41:19.800 --> 00:41:21.595
Which is really valuable. So for example,

00:41:21.595 --> 00:41:24.450
the way our UNHCR
partnership works right now,

00:41:24.450 --> 00:41:27.600
we figured out that they're not
particularly useful in terms

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:30.660
of like statistical decision
sampling frames, et cetera.

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:33.630
But the, the UNHCR country
representative is very,

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:37.860
very useful in telling us sort
of keeping the pulse of like,

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:40.680
what is it that we need to
know in the next round, right?

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.080
So essentially the partnership
we have is that they tell us,

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:43.913
okay,

00:41:43.913 --> 00:41:46.050
here are the policy questions
that need to be answered so

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:49.890
that our research team can be
responsive exactly to that.

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.830
And here I should, I'm
here on stage talking,

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:56.100
but the person who's overtime
taken on the lead PI role,

00:41:56.100 --> 00:41:57.180
Paola Lopez-Pena,

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:00.060
she's a Queens University and
she has been maintaining a lot

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:01.950
of those relationships. Thank you.

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:02.850
I'll stop there.

00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:06.480
- Thanks very much.
(audience clapping)

